export const body = "<p>The success of the early <em>Resident Evil </em>games can partly be attributed to the fixed camera style, which not only heightened the tension during moment-to-moment gameplay but also allowed for intentional framing. In the 1998 game, when players arrive at the police station, they shortly encounter a new monster skittering outside the window, foreshadowing imminent danger. Thanks to the static camera, the developers could guarantee this reveal would always play out exactly the way they want.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/ad07742575e614eb3307.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/ad07742575e614eb3307.webp\" width=\"928\" height=\"714\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/b2a15362553a87163f63.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/b2a15362553a87163f63.webp\" width=\"934\" height=\"716\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/0c82380b1d50afa71645.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/0c82380b1d50afa71645.webp\" width=\"763\" height=\"425\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>In the modern era of gaming, the camera is often handed to the player, so developers have to evolve their framing technique. Continuing with<em> Resident Evil 2</em>, let's see how the remake recaptured the iconic reveal of the Licker.</p>\n\n<p>In the GIF, you can see that the player is funneled through a straight hallway as they approach the window. The geometry of the level essentially directs the player's attention to the window where the main event is framed.</p>\n\n<p>This \"funneling\" technique works best with corridors and hallways and bridges, just about every straight piece of geometry you can have in a level. These framing devices often have symmetrical construction, so they inherently encourage players to speed forward as there are no distinct visuals on either side for them to linger on.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/a461ddc5f28995dab363.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/a461ddc5f28995dab363.webp\" width=\"901\" height=\"507\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/0f0ce8207292df51f9c7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/0f0ce8207292df51f9c7.webp\" width=\"901\" height=\"507\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/10da57bcb38791c3c60b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/10da57bcb38791c3c60b.webp\" width=\"637\" height=\"355\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>However, narrow pathways are not always available, so we have to find other ways approaches like this moment from <em>God of War</em>. As Kratos climbs through the cavity, the camera tilts sideways and then zooms in to frame the scene on the other side. Because sequences like this require some degree of coordination with the camera system, it largely depends on how your game's camera behaves.</p>\n\n<p>In cases where your environments are too spacious for \"funneling,\" you can leverage negative space to create contrast and, in turn, steer the player's attention on your desired location. In the top picture, the contrast between the darkened architecture and the white snow piling on the ground shrinks our vision, guiding our eyes to the doorway where a hooded figure is standing.</p>\n\n<p>In the bottom picture, the geometric formation also is veiled in shadow, lending contrast to the sunset landscape. If you look at the minimap (on the top right), you can find an objective marker beckoning to the player, which means he will automatically turn to the direction at which the scenery beyond the gate is framed. This ties into the next and final point in this article.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/78456ee75afd111f8cfb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/78456ee75afd111f8cfb.webp\" width=\"832\" height=\"520\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/3b89cc146c0840f7d77a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/3b89cc146c0840f7d77a.webp\" width=\"832\" height=\"468\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/649f6632ee7eec918b57.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/649f6632ee7eec918b57.webp\" width=\"874\" height=\"478\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/7b48e66c70ad9dd71d36.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/7b48e66c70ad9dd71d36.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Being a stealth action game, <em>The Last of Us 2 </em>has an investigative combat loop, one that demands its players to survey the battlefield and think ahead. Although there are times when enemies besiege the player in swarms, the majority of the gameplay revolves around sneaking between covers and executing the opposition one by one. With this playstyle established, the game effectively attracts players to covers, opening up many opportunities for framing like the moment in the GIF.</p>\n\n<p>Likewise, <em>Sekiro </em>has a semi-cover system which prompts the player character to lean against a wall and snoop on the other side, turning half of the screen into a frame.</p>\n\n<p>In conclusion, framing is a composition technique that is not restricted to the fixed camera style. Games with dynamic camera systems still can employ this technique by manipulating level geometry and negative space. Lastly, understanding player behavior is also a gateway to ingenious framing during gameplay.</p>\n\n<p>Thank you for reading and happy framing!</p>";
